  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Saving the Sumatran elephant through supporting resort-based management in Gunung Leuser National Park, Indonesia to reduce encroachment and prevent poaching</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The goal of this three-year project is to assist Indonesian authorities in improving the management of Gunung Leuser National Park (GLNP), Sumatra, Indonesia to protect Critically Endangered Sumatran elephants and other wildlife. Objectives focus on the prevention of threats and protection of habitat and elephants through patrol and direct monitoring in the field in alignment with national park priorities. Project activities include: 1) strengthening GLNP through the integration of SMART into forest patrols to reduce forest encroachment and other threats in areas with key elephant populations; 2) support government law enforcement agencies to prosecute forest crimes detected by SMART patrols; and 3) combat illegal trade in elephant parts through investigating and prosecuting wildlife traders. This project will address the three main threats to wild elephants in GLNP - poaching and illegal wildlife trade, HEC, and deforestation - and contribute to the conservation of wild elephants in Sumatra, Indonesia. This award is provisionally approved for three phases or years funding, with each phase or year&apos;s funding dependent upon performance and financial management of the previous phase or year, meeting technical and financial reporting requirements, and availability of ASECF funds.</narrative>
  </description>
